1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,770
Chai Corner has six people now,
and Ravi loves one way to manage.

2
00:00:05,070 --> 00:00:08,131
Long team chats, where
everyone shares ideas.

3
00:00:08,681 --> 00:00:11,131
On a slow afternoon, it works beautifully.

4
00:00:11,431 --> 00:00:15,355
But at eight in the morning, the rush
hits, and Ravi calls a team chat.

5
00:00:15,655 --> 00:00:18,573
Thirty customers wait,
and their chai goes cold.

6
00:00:19,223 --> 00:00:21,064
There is no one best way.

7
00:00:21,064 --> 00:00:22,232
It all depends.

8
00:00:22,932 --> 00:00:25,208
This is the contingency approach.

9
00:00:25,508 --> 00:00:27,983
Contingent means depending on something.

10
00:00:28,333 --> 00:00:32,588
Earlier thinkers, like Taylor,
searched for one best way for everyone.

11
00:00:32,938 --> 00:00:37,148
This approach says the right way
to manage depends on the situation.

12
00:00:37,798 --> 00:00:42,856
The idea grew in the 1960s and 1970s.

13
00:00:43,156 --> 00:00:48,626
In 1961, Burns and Stalker found
that stable firms do well with strict

14
00:00:48,626 --> 00:00:52,800
rules, and fast changing
firms need flexible teams.

15
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:58,556
In 1965, Joan Woodward showed that the
best structure depends on a factory's

16
00:00:58,556 --> 00:00:59,549
technology.

17
00:00:59,899 --> 00:01:04,951
In 1967, Lawrence and Lorsch showed
that it depends on the environment

18
00:01:04,951 --> 00:01:05,851
too.

19
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:10,278
And Fred Fiedler showed that the
best leader depends on the situation.

20
00:01:10,928 --> 00:01:13,915
Contingency thinking uses one simple tool.

21
00:01:13,915 --> 00:01:14,929
If, then.

22
00:01:15,279 --> 00:01:18,436
If the situation is like
this, then manage like that.

23
00:01:18,786 --> 00:01:19,686
Size.

24
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:22,388
Six people need fewer rules than sixty.

25
00:01:22,688 --> 00:01:23,588
Technology.

26
00:01:23,569 --> 00:01:26,416
That means the tools and methods you use.

27
00:01:26,716 --> 00:01:27,690
Environment.

28
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:30,301
Customers, prices, and competitors.

29
00:01:30,301 --> 00:01:32,271
Stable, or changing fast?

30
00:01:32,571 --> 00:01:33,512
And people.

31
00:01:33,512 --> 00:01:35,724
A new helper needs clear steps.

32
00:01:35,724 --> 00:01:37,454
An expert needs freedom.

33
00:01:38,104 --> 00:01:41,140
Back at Chai Corner, Ravi
tries if-then thinking.

34
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:45,429
If it's the morning rush, then he
gives quick, firm instructions.

35
00:01:45,429 --> 00:01:47,937
Asha brews, Kabir bills, Meena cleans.

36
00:01:48,287 --> 00:01:52,534
If it's a slow afternoon, then
the team relaxes and shares ideas.

37
00:01:52,884 --> 00:01:54,527
Same team, same stall.

38
00:01:54,527 --> 00:01:56,422
Two styles, and both work.

39
00:01:57,072 --> 00:01:59,638
Ravi is also planning a second outlet.

40
00:01:59,938 --> 00:02:04,251
Near a college, it needs fast
service, low prices, and a fun feel.

41
00:02:04,601 --> 00:02:08,523
Near an office, it needs orders
on time, and quiet tables.

42
00:02:08,873 --> 00:02:11,918
One brand, two places,
two different approaches.

43
00:02:12,568 --> 00:02:13,468
Quick check.

44
00:02:13,468 --> 00:02:15,612
The morning rush hits Chai Corner.

45
00:02:15,612 --> 00:02:17,662
Which style fits best right now?

46
00:02:19,562 --> 00:02:21,163
Quick, firm instructions!

47
00:02:21,163 --> 00:02:23,529
A rush needs speed, not a long chat.

48
00:02:24,129 --> 00:02:25,138
So, remember.

49
00:02:25,438 --> 00:02:27,848
There is no one best way to manage.

50
00:02:27,848 --> 00:02:29,997
It all depends on the situation.

51
00:02:30,347 --> 00:02:34,561
Size, technology, environment,
and people shape the right choice.

52
00:02:34,911 --> 00:02:36,144
And think if-then.

53
00:02:36,144 --> 00:02:38,658
If this situation, then that approach.

54
00:02:39,258 --> 00:02:43,406
Next time, we'll explore the core
jobs of every manager, the functions

55
00:02:43,406 --> 00:02:44,410
of management.
